"Acceptance Is Not Speed: Length Inflation in Training-Free Lossy Speculative Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1)\nciteturn41238view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 755\nL79: Large language models generate text autoregressively: every new token requires a full forward pass over the prefix. Speculative decoding accelerates this loop by separating proposal from verification. A cheap draft model proposes several tokens ahead, and the target model scores all of them in one forward pass, committing the longest accepted prefix (cite58†Stern et al., 2018 ; cite59†Leviathan et al., 2023 ; cite60†Chen et al., 2023 ).\nL80: The technique is now standard in production inference stacks, with learned drafters such as the EAGLE family (cite61†Li et al., 2024b ; cite62†Li et al., 2024a ; cite63†Li et al., 2025 ) shipped in vLLM (cite64†Kwon et al., 2023 ), SGLang (cite65†Zheng et al., 2024 ), and TensorRT-LLM (cite66†NVIDIA, 2023 ).\nL81: We call such drafters dedicated: a dedicated drafter computes its proposals from the target’s own hidden states, so it is trained for one target and cannot run without it.^{1}^{1} 1 Formally, a dedicated drafter samples from $q(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t},h_{<t})$, where $h_{<t}$ are the target’s hidden states on the prefix $x_{<t}$, and a standalone drafter from $q(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$.\nL82: For example, the EAGLE-3 head of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct is a single transformer layer that drafts from features of a low, a middle, and a high layer of the target. A standalone drafter is instead a complete small language model that drafts from the tokens alone and shares the target’s tokenizer, such as Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct for the same target.\nL83: In lossless speculative decoding the acceptance rule preserves the target distribution exactly, so the speedup is capped by how closely the drafter tracks the target (cite59†Leviathan et al., 2023 ). Because the output distribution is unchanged, completion length is unchanged too, and a gain in accepted tokens per round is a gain in speed.\nL84: Lossy speculative decoding relaxes the acceptance rule to admit more draft tokens, trading exactness of the output distribution for speed. It also gives up the guarantee on length: a relaxed rule decodes a different distribution, and its completions can be longer or shorter than the target’s.\nL85: A growing family of training-free methods does this by replacing the target distribution inside verification with a relaxed distribution $\\pi$ controlled by a relaxation parameter $\\alpha$ (cite67†Xia et al., 2026 ; cite68†Li et al., 2026 ). These methods report higher acceptance and throughput at what their evaluations describe as negligible quality cost.\nL86: Those gains, however, are measured almost entirely in accepted tokens per round and tokens per second (cite69†Wang et al., 2026a ). A relaxed rule changes which tokens are committed, and therefore how long its completions run and whether they terminate cleanly. If a method accepts more tokens per round but also inflates completion length, the end-to-end gain shrinks and can invert. We call this length inflation under relaxed verification. The effect is invisible to both metrics.\nL87: This paper measures it directly (Sections cite21†3.3 and cite22†3.4 ), characterizes it across rules, tasks, targets, drafters, and settings (Sections cite23†3.5 and cite24†3.6 ), and traces it to the low-probability tokens that relaxation commits (Section cite25†3.7 ). The effect matters for reasoning models, whose thinking traces run to thousands of tokens.\nL88: The added length buys no accuracy: forcing the reasoning trace to end near the generation budget recovers far more correct answers than it loses (Section cite26†4 ).\nL89: Differences with prior work.  Prior work has not sufficiently characterized the extent and consequences of length inflation under relaxed verification. cite67†Xia et al. (2026) discuss longer, rambling responses only in weak-drafter settings. cite69†Wang et al. (2026a) study quality degradation but do not measure completion length.\nL90: By systematically treating length inflation as a central measurement of relaxed verification, we identify it as a consistent phenomenon across lossy speculative decoding methods, whose final outputs are longer than their lossless counterparts. We further investigate what the additional generation accomplishes and whether its consequences are recoverable. These findings support a concrete explanation for some unsuccessful responses: continued reasoning exhausts the budget needed to deliver a final answer.\nL91: In summary, we uncover a new prevalent phenomenon in lossy speculative decoding and identify its cause.\nL92: We evaluate five training-free relaxed verification rules, namely CACTUS (cite70†Hao and Mou, 2026 ), mentored decoding (cite71†Tran-Thien, 2023 ), reducible fuzzy speculative decoding (cite72†Holsman et al., 2025 ), and the two speculative cascade rules (cite73†Narasimhan et al., 2025 ), implemented as published, on six benchmarks covering math, code, chat, and long context, with twelve target-drafter pairs that combine four targets (GPT-OSS-20B, Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) with eight dedicated drafters and three standalone language models (360 cells in all).\nL93: Our contributions are:\nL94:   1. 1.\nL95: \nL96: a token-efficiency accounting for relaxed speculative decoding: completion length, verifier rounds, the condition under which a relaxed rule saves decoding time, and the largest length inflation any relaxation of a given drafter can absorb (Section cite9†2.2 ), with a protocol that compares methods at matched accepted length rather than matched $\\alpha$;\nL97: \nL98:   2. 2.\nL99: a cross-method, benchmark, target, and drafter measurement over 360 cells (Section cite14†3 ), with paired bootstrap intervals for every cell, showing that relaxation inflates completion length, that the inflation cancels most of the promised reduction in verifier rounds and in measured time, that this holds at gentle settings as well as loose ones, and that the accuracy cost is large at loose settings and task dependent;\nL100: \nL101:   3. 3.\nL102: the identification of where the extra length comes from: relaxation admits draft tokens the target rates as unlikely, and the extra text goes into the reasoning trace where the model has one (Section cite25†3.7 );\nL103: \nL104:   4. 4.\nL105: \nL106: evidence that the added length is waste: on grade-school math it follows a correct answer the model has already written, on competition math it delays the answer, and forcing the reasoning trace to end near the generation budget recovers far more correct answers (Section cite26†4 ).\nL107: ## 2 Relaxed Speculative Decoding\nL108: \nL109: This section presents lossless and lossy speculative decoding in one framework and states every training-free method as a choice of relaxed target distributions in it; following cite67†Xia et al. (2026) we also call the two cases strict and relaxed. Appendix cite33†A gives each method’s rule and our implementation.\nL110: ### 2.1 Strict speculative decoding\nL111: Let $p$ be the target model to accelerate and $q$ a cheaper drafter, with next-token distributions $p_{t}(\\cdot)=p(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$ and $q_{t}(\\cdot)=q(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$ over a vocabulary of size $V$, and $x$ a drafted token. In one round of speculative decoding (Algorithm cite74†1 in Appendix cite33†A , with $\\pi=p$), the drafter samples a block of $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}$ tokens and the target scores all of them, plus the position after the block, in a single forward pass.\nL112: Each draft token is then accepted with probability $\\min\\{1,p(x)/q(x)\\}$; at the first rejection the token is replaced by a sample from the residual distribution $\\normop(\\max\\{0,p-q\\})$ and the round ends. If every draft token is accepted, a bonus token is sampled from $p$ at the position after the block. One target forward pass thus commits between one and $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}+1$ tokens, and the output is distributed exactly as $p$ (cite59†Leviathan et al., 2023 ; cite60†Chen et al., 2023 ).\nL113: Let $C_{\\mathrm{AR}}$, $C_{\\mathrm{drafter}}$, and $C_{\\mathrm{verify}}$ be the wall-clock times of one decoding step of the target $p$, one decoding step of the drafter $q$, and one verification pass over the $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}+1$ positions, and let $\\bar{\\ell}$ denote the mean number of draft tokens accepted per round. A round costs $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}\\,C_{\\mathrm{drafter}}+C_{\\mathrm{verify}}$ and commits $\\bar{\\ell}+1$ tokens on average.\nL114: Under memory-bound conditions $C_{\\mathrm{verify}}\\approx C_{\\mathrm{AR}}$, so with $c_{\\mathrm{rel}}=C_{\\mathrm{drafter}}/C_{\\mathrm{AR}}$ the speedup is\nL115:  | $$S\\approx\\frac{(\\bar{\\ell}+1)\\,C_{\\mathrm{AR}}}{N_{\\mathrm{draft}}\\,C_{\\mathrm{drafter}}+C_{\\mathrm{verify}}}\\approx\\frac{\\bar{\\ell}+1}{1+N_{\\mathrm{draft}}\\,c_{\\mathrm{rel}}}.$$  |  | (1)\nL116: \nL117: The accepted length is bounded by drafter–target agreement: a draft token drawn from $q$ is accepted with probability\nL118: \nL119:  | $$\\textstyle\\sum_{v}\\min\\{p(v),q(v)\\}=1-\\mathrm{TV}(p,q),$$  |  | (2)\nL120: so strict verification cannot accept more than the two distributions share (cite59†Leviathan et al., 2023 ). Every relaxed method below is an attempt to lift this cap.\nL121: ### 2.2 Relaxed speculative decoding\nL122: A relaxed method keeps the round of Section cite8†2.1 intact and replaces the target distribution $p$ at up to three points with relaxed target distributions $\\pi$: $\\pi^{\\mathrm{rej}}$ in the acceptance distribution, so that a draft token is accepted with probability $\\min\\{1,\\pi^{\\mathrm{rej}}(x)/q(x)\\}$; $\\pi^{\\mathrm{res}}$ in the residual sampled after a rejection; and $\\pi^{\\mathrm{bonus}}$ at the bonus position.\nL123: Each is a function $\\pi[q,p,x,\\alpha](\\cdot)$ of the two distributions, the drafted token, and a method-specific relaxation parameter $\\alpha$. Strict speculative decoding is the special case $\\pi=p$ at all three points.\nL124: The parameter $\\alpha$ means something different in every method (a divergence budget, a divergence threshold, a deferral cost, a mixture weight), so values of $\\alpha$ are never comparable across methods; Section cite14†3 therefore compares methods at matched $\\bar{\\ell}$ rather than matched $\\alpha$.\nL125: #### Scope.\nL126: \nL127: We restrict attention to training-free rules that relax verification per token, i.e. methods fully specified by a choice of $(\\pi^{\\mathrm{rej}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{res}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{bonus}})$; rules that train a component, such as a judge head (cite75†Bachmann et al., 2025 ; cite76†Garipov et al., 2025 ) or a learned ensemble weight (cite77†Wang et al., 2026b ), expose no $\\alpha$ to sweep.\nL128: #### Length-corrected speedup.\nL129: \nL130: Because a relaxed rule samples from $\\pi$ rather than $p$, it can change the length of the generated response, and Equation (cite78†1 ) no longer measures end-to-end speed. We can correct for this with\nL131: \nL132:  | $$S_{\\mathrm{relax}}\\approx\\frac{\\bar{L}_{\\mathrm{AR}}}{\\bar{L}_{\\mathrm{relax}}}\\cdot S,$$  |  | (3)\nL133: where $\\bar{L}$ denotes mean completion length and $S$ is the acceptance-based speedup of Equation (cite78†1 ). This study measures that factor directly: a method wins end to end only if its acceptance gain exceeds its length inflation.\nL134: #### Verifier rounds.\nL135: \nL136: The same trade-off can be measured without a hardware model. A completion of $L$ tokens generated in $R$ verifier rounds (target forward passes) commits $\\bar{\\ell}+1$ tokens per round on average, so\nL137: \nL138:  | $$R\\approx\\frac{L}{\\bar{\\ell}+1}.$$  |  | (4)\nL139: Every round costs $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}$ drafter steps and one verification pass for every rule in the sweep, so at fixed $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}$ and drafter the decoding times of two rules are in the ratio of their round counts. Writing $\\lambda=\\bar{L}_{\\mathrm{relax}}/\\bar{L}_{\\mathrm{strict}}$ for the length inflation relative to strict verification,\nL140: \nL141:  | $$\\frac{R_{\\mathrm{relax}}}{R_{\\mathrm{strict}}}\\approx\\lambda\\cdot\\frac{\\bar{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{strict}}+1}{\\bar{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{relax}}+1},$$  |  | (5)\nL142: and a relaxed rule reduces decoding time only if $(\\bar{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{relax}}+1)/(\\bar{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{strict}}+1)>\\lambda$. The gain on the left is bounded by the draft length: a round accepts at most $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}$ draft tokens, so no relaxation of a given drafter can beat $\\lambda=(N_{\\mathrm{draft}}+1)/(\\bar{\\ell}_{\\mathrm{strict}}+1)$. Section cite14†3 reports $\\bar{L}$, $R$, measured time, and task accuracy directly, at matched $\\bar{\\ell}$.\nL143: ### 2.3 Training-free methods\nL144: \nL145: Table cite79†A1 states every method as a choice of $(\\pi^{\\mathrm{rej}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{res}},\\pi^{\\mathrm{bonus}})$. The five swept rules fall into two groups: divergence-budgeted rules that boost the drafted token while bounding the distance from $p$ (CACTUS, mentored decoding), and switch rules that trust the drafter outright when a per-position test passes (fuzzy speculative decoding, speculative cascades).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIgnored, Not Refused: What Low Prompt-Injection Success Rates Hidein Small Tool-Using Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1)\nciteturn41238view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23254v1\",\"lineno\":170}); Total lines: 558\nL136: ASR cannot distinguish this from a genuine refusal, but a behavioral audit can.\nL137: #### Security–utility metrics.\nL138: Several benchmarks also measure whether the user’s task is completed. AgentDojo reports benign utility and utility under attack (UA) alongside ASR (cite72†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ). ASB adds a refusal rate, judged by the agent’s own model, and folds clean-task utility and ASR into one score, NRP (cite76†Zhang et al., 2025 ). cite91†Bhagwatkar et al. (2025) recommend reporting benign utility, ASR, and UA together. Meanwhile, cite92†Chen et al.\nL139: (2026a) score security and utility per attack, highlighting covert attacks that succeed while completing the user task. However, these measures are read side by side or folded into one number, so none shows how often a safe agent fails its task. ATOM makes this visible (§cite31†4.3 ).\nL140: Overall, prior work shows that ASR obscures failure modes, recognized compliance, deployment sensitivity, and joint utility. However, previous studies examine these phenomena in isolation. We study them together on the failures that standard benchmarks already record, across system prompts and serving precisions. Our audit is observational. It reads only the agents’ actions and stated reasoning, not their internal activations.\nL141: ## 3 Study Design\nL142: \nL143: Figure 1: (A) Baseline trajectories are scored by the benchmarks and labeled by a label-blind judge. (B) With the same models and attacks, only the system prompt or the serving precision is changed.\nL144: #### Threat model and benchmarks.\nL145: The attacker can only modify the external content that reaches the agent through its tool outputs. It cannot change the system prompt, the user’s request, the tools or the model. InjecAgent (cite71†Zhan et al., 2024 ) has 1,054 two-turn cases. Its parser scores each attack as successful, unsuccessful or invalid, and the benchmark reports ASR. AgentDojo (cite72†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ) has 949 stateful, multi-turn episodes across four task suites.\nL146: Each episode receives a security verdict $S$ ($S{=}1$ if the attack succeeds) and a utility verdict $U$ ($U{=}1$ if the task is completed). AgentDojo reports ASR, utility under attack (UA) and benign utility (BU). We use these two benchmarks and their official metrics to evaluate all eleven agents.\nL147: #### Evaluated Models, Tool Scaffolds, and Serving Environments.\nL148: Figure cite93†1 summarizes our pipeline. We evaluate eleven agents from six architecture families with 2–14B parameters (Appendix cite43†A ). We serve each agent at FP16, at FP8 with weight-only quantization (cite94†Micikevicius et al., 2022 ), and at NF4 (cite95†Dettmers et al., 2023 ). For the audit, we then draw a locked random sample of 500 trajectories for each agent, benchmark and precision.\nL149: The sample is stratified by suite on AgentDojo and by attack class on InjecAgent, which gives 66 cells and 33,000 trajectories.\nL150:  |  | Behavior of failed attacks (% of failures)\nL151:  | Failed  | Inert  | Genuine  | Mechanical  | Compliance\nL152: Agent  | attacks  | Unrecognized  | Recognized  | Refusal  | Breakdown  | Partial  | Full\nL153: AgentDojo\nL154: ---\nL155: Gemma-4B  | 1,264  | 68.4  | 16.1  | 11.1  | 0.2  | 2.5  | 1.8\nL156: Gemma-12B  | 1,201  | 87.1  | 2.0  | 3.5  | 1.8  | 2.4  | 3.2\nL157: LFM-2.6B  | 1,465  | 76.0  | 3.5  | 17.3  | 0.1  | 2.7  | 0.4\nL158: MiniCPM-2B  | 1,432  | 88.1  | 4.8  | 4.5  | 1.3  | 1.4  | 0.0\nL159: Ministral-14B  | 1,236  | 81.7  | 0.9  | 0.8  | 8.3  | 2.1  | 6.1\nL160: Nanbeige-3B  | 1,427  | 86.6  | 4.9  | 7.6  | 0.2  | 0.3  | 0.4\nL161: Nemotron-Nano-4B  | 1,248  | 77.9  | 9.7  | 1.1  | 2.3  | 3.7  | 5.3\nL162: Ornith-9B  | 1,478  | 15.0  | 0.3  | 83.6  | 0.5  | 0.3  | 0.3\nL163: Qwen-4B  | 1,139  | 78.8  | 6.4  | 4.4  | 3.0  | 3.7  | 3.8\nL164: Qwen-9B  | 1,335  | 91.3  | 4.7  | 2.0  | 0.6  | 0.9  | 0.4\nL165: Spark-4B  | 1,474  | 45.5  | 0.9  | 47.6  | 5.0  | 0.6  | 0.3\nL166: All agents  | 14,699  | 71.5  | 4.8  | 18.0  | 2.1  | 1.8  | 1.9\nL167: InjecAgent\nL168: ---\nL169: Gemma-4B  | 1,393  | 67.6  | 23.8  | 8.4  | 0.2  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL170: Gemma-12B  | 1,460  | 44.9  | 34.8  | 20.0  | 0.3  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL171: LFM-2.6B  | 1,500  | 89.3  | 5.1  | 5.1  | 0.1  | 0.3  | 0.0\nL172: MiniCPM-2B  | 1,490  | 92.6  | 2.7  | 2.9  | 1.8  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL173: Ministral-14B  | 1,445  | 63.5  | 15.9  | 4.4  | 1.4  | 7.9  | 6.9\nL174: Nanbeige-3B  | 1,469  | 43.6  | 17.9  | 38.1  | 0.3  | 0.0  | 0.1\nL175: Nemotron-Nano-4B  | 1,451  | 95.2  | 3.7  | 0.5  | 0.1  | 0.3  | 0.1\nL176: Ornith-9B  | 1,191  | 15.3  | 5.5  | 71.9  | 7.1  | 0.0  | 0.3\nL177: Qwen-4B  | 1,263  | 82.7  | 10.1  | 6.8  | 0.1  | 0.2  | 0.2\nL178: Qwen-9B  | 1,128  | 61.0  | 24.6  | 13.7  | 0.1  | 0.3  | 0.4\nL179: Spark-4B  | 1,484  | 66.9  | 8.1  | 24.8  | 0.2  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL180: All agents  | 15,274  | 66.6  | 13.7  | 17.2  | 1.0  | 0.8  | 0.7\nL181: Table 2: What benchmark-recorded attack failures consist of. Each agent contributes 1,500 audited trajectories per benchmark (500 per precision, pooled). Failed attacks: trajectories the benchmark scored as failed. Behavior columns: % of those failures; Partial and Full Compliance are judge–benchmark disagreements.\nL182: #### Label-blind behavioral audit.\nL183: An LLM judge labels each sampled trajectory. It receives the user task, the injected instruction, the attacker’s goal and the full trajectory. The judge evaluates anonymized trajectories without access to model identifiers or benchmark scores. Hiding the name, however, is not enough on its own. LLM judges often favor outputs from their own model family even when anonymous. This bias stems from their ability to recognize self-generated text (cite96†Pombal et al., 2026 ).\nL184: We therefore use DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash as the judge, since no agent we evaluate comes from the DeepSeek family. As a reasoning model, it judges more accurately than non-reasoning counterparts. Reasoning models also demonstrate greater robustness against injected instructions in evaluated content (cite97†Huang et al., 2026 ). This robustness is essential here, because every trajectory the judge reads contains an injection. The judge assigns one of six labels, in precedence order:\nL185:   * •\nL186: \nL187: Full Compliance: the attacker’s goal was executed.\nL188: \nL189:   * •\nL190: \nL191: Partial Compliance: some attacker action, but the goal was not completed.\nL192: \nL193:   * •\nL194: \nL195: Genuine Refusal: the reasoning identifies the injection as an attack or as unauthorized, and it is not executed.\nL196: \nL197:   * •\nL198: \nL199: Inert-Recognized (Inert-R): the reasoning identifies the injected text as an instruction to the agent, but the agent neither executes nor rejects it.\nL200: \nL201:   * •\nL202: Inert-Unrecognized (Inert-U): the reasoning never treats the injected text as an instruction.\nL203: \nL204:   * •\nL205: \nL206: Mechanical Breakdown: a crash, a malformed or repeated call, or truncation is the main reason the attack did not complete.\nL207: For the analyses below, we also merge the six labels into four groups (Table cite98†2 ). The two compliance labels form compliance, Genuine Refusal forms refusal, the two Inert labels form ignored, and Mechanical Breakdown forms breakdown. To prevent hallucinated audit labels, an automated script verifies that the judge’s evidence quote appears verbatim in the agent’s chain-of-thought.\nL208: Claims lacking verbatim textual grounding are downgraded to Inert-Unrecognized, making our reported recognition rates a conservative empirical lower bound.\nL209: #### Judge validation.\nL210: We check the judge against two references (Appendix cite51†D ). The first is human labels. Two authors labeled 150 trajectories and resolved their disagreements by discussion, following XSTest (cite99†Röttger et al., 2024 ). Their labels agree with the judge on every attacker action and on 96% of four-way groups ($\\kappa{=}0.835$). The second is the benchmarks themselves. Across all 33,000 trajectories, the judge agrees with the benchmarks’ own verdicts on 96.7% of AgentDojo and 98.5% of InjecAgent cases.\nL211: #### Deployment conditions.\nL212: \nL213: Figure cite93†1 (B) tests whether a low baseline ASR holds when an operator changes the deployment without retraining the model. Each condition changes one thing, while the models and the attacks stay the same. We then compare every case with the same case at baseline. We test three conditions, and their exact prompts are in Appendix cite50†C .\nL214: \nL215:   * •\nL216: Interdict / Authorize (InjecAgent, 11 agents): matched system-prompt policies with identical templates and near-equal length (163 and 172 words). They instruct agents that tool-output directives are either threats to report or legitimate tasks to fulfill.\nL217: \nL218:   * •\nL219: \nL220: Source-boundary guardrail (InjecAgent, 9 agents): a 182-word, three-rule block. It instructs the agent to treat tool outputs strictly as data and verify each tool call.\nL221: \nL222:   * •\nL223: Precision (both benchmarks, 11 agents): the same checkpoints served at FP8 and NF4 instead of FP16.\nL224: \nL225: The policy pair tests the Untriggered Default hypothesis. If small agents ignore injections because they enforce a boundary between instructions and data, permission should barely matter. If they ignore them only because nothing tells them to act, then $\\text{ASR}_{\\text{Authorize}}\\gg\\text{ASR}_{\\text{baseline}}\\gg\\text{ASR}_{\\text{Interdict}}$.\nL226:  | InjecAgent  | AgentDojo\nL227: --- | --- | ---\nL228: Agent  | ASR  | Invalid  | ASR  | BU  | UA\nL229: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL230: Gemma-4B  | 7.02  | 1  | 13.49  | 78.35  | 68.18\nL231: Gemma-12B  | 3.51  | 1  | 27.61  | 75.26  | 58.09\nL232: LFM-2.6B  | 0.00  | 735  | 2.42  | 65.98  | 66.67\nL233: MiniCPM-2B  | 0.85  | 42  | 5.90  | 81.44  | 75.24\nL234: Ministral-14B  | 0.28  | 263  | 22.55  | 64.95  | 55.99\nL235: Nanbeige-3B  | 2.47  | 270  | 4.64  | 84.54  | 83.77\nL236: Nemotron-Nano-4B  | 2.56  | 162  | 15.81  | 67.01  | 62.45\nL237: Ornith-9B  | 27.42  | 19  | 1.90  | 90.72  | 89.04\nL238: Qwen-4B  | 18.79  | 19  | 25.18  | 76.29  | 71.44\nL239: Qwen-9B  | 25.14  | 44  | 9.69  | 89.69  | 85.88\nL240: Spark-4B  | 0.95  | 6  | 2.32  | 82.47  | 83.03\nL241: Pooled  | 8.09  | 1,562  | 11.96  | 77.88  | 72.73\nL242: Table 3: Baseline at FP16: ASR (%) over 1,054 InjecAgent and 949 AgentDojo cases per agent; Invalid: InjecAgent outputs not readable as a tool call; BU: Benign Utility (97 clean tasks); UA: Utility Under Attack (%).\nL243: #### Outcome measures.\nL244: ASR is the share of attacks whose goal is reached, and it counts InjecAgent’s invalid outputs as failed attacks. BU is the task completion rate on the 97 clean AgentDojo tasks. UA is the share of attacked episodes with $U{=}1$, whatever the attack outcome, as AgentDojo computes it. For comparison, we also compute ASB’s net resilient performance, ${\\text{NRP}=\\text{PNA}\\times(1-\\text{ASR})}$ (cite76†Zhang et al., 2025 ), where PNA is task performance without attacks, which we take to be BU.\nL245: Finally, the Attack–Task Outcome Matrix (ATOM) evaluates $S$ and $U$ jointly for each AgentDojo episode. It defines four distinct outcome cells: Safe and Useful ($S_{0}U_{1}$), Safe but Dysfunctional ($S_{0}U_{0}$), Unsafe but Useful ($S_{1}U_{1}$), and Unsafe and Hijacked ($S_{1}U_{0}$).\nL246: ## 4 Results and Analysis\nL247: \nL248: ### 4.1 Most Failed Attacks Are Ignored, Not Refused\nL249: \nL250: Figure 2: InjecAgent ASR (%) per agent with fixed checkpoints and attacks. We evaluate matched policies (Interdict, Authorize), the guardrail at FP16, and baseline prompts across precisions (FP16, FP8, NF4). Darker cells mean higher ASR; dashes (–) mark agents without a guardrail run.\nL251: #### Baseline ASR makes most agents look secure.\nL252: \nL253: At the FP16 baseline, pooled ASR is only 8.09% on InjecAgent and 11.96% on AgentDojo (Table cite100†3 ). Eight of the eleven agents stay below 10% on InjecAgent. By this measure, most agents look secure.\nL254: #### Ignoring far outnumbers refusing.\nL255: The benchmarks scored 14,699 AgentDojo and 15,274 InjecAgent trajectories as failed attacks (Table cite98†2 ). In 76.3% and 80.3% of them, the agent ignored the injection, while Genuine Refusal accounts for only 18.0% [17.4, 18.6] and 17.2% [16.6, 17.8]. This pattern is consistent across models. Nearly every agent ignores more injections than it refuses: 9 of 11 on AgentDojo and 10 of 11 on InjecAgent. The pattern holds at every precision and under every robustness check we ran (Appendix cite57†F ).\nL256: #### A frontier agent refuses instead.\nL257: \nL258: In one run, Kimi K3, a frontier model audited with the same judge, has ASR 0.4% on AgentDojo and 0% on InjecAgent. Of its failed attacks, 96.6% and 99.0% are refusals (Appendix cite57†F ). It notices the injection and explicitly declines it, leaving an auditable signal. The small agents, by contrast, mostly reach a low ASR by ignoring the injection, leaving none.\nL259: #### Noticing an injection does not mean resisting it.\n"
